import pf.Html
import ../../../Ui/Heading
import ../../../Ui/Callout
import ../../../Ui/Code
import ../../../Generated/Sources
import ../../../Facts
import ../../../Generated/Paths
import ../../../Ui/PageHero
import ../../../Ui/TableWrap
import ../../../Ui/Trace
import ../../../Ui/Who
import ../../../Ui/Arch
import ../../../Ui/ArchBox

meta = {
	title: "The browser",
	description: "The rocx browser platform: an app's islands compiled to WebAssembly, a Zig host, and a JavaScript runtime that hydrates them and runs their holes again when signals change. With the numbers this build measured.",
	toc: [
		{ id: "parts", label: "The parts" },
		{ id: "header", label: "The header" },
		{ id: "click", label: "One click, all the way" },
		{ id: "hydration", label: "Hydration" },
		{ id: "waiting", label: "Waiting, in wasm" },
		{ id: "numbers", label: "The numbers" },
		{ id: "memory", label: "Memory" },
	],
}

view! = |_| {
	<>
		<NoHydration>
			<PageHero
				eyebrow="The platform · the browser"
				title="The islands in WebAssembly, a Zig host, and a page of JavaScript"
			>
				The browser's half of an app is its islands: the components, compiled to one wasm module. Roc
				decides what each hole shows and what each handler does; a runtime in JavaScript keeps the
				graph of what reads what, runs holes again when what they read changes, and touches the DOM.
			</PageHero>

			<Heading id="parts">The parts</Heading>
			<Arch
				left={[
					<ArchBox kind="js" name={[<code>web/runtime.js</code>]}>
						<p>
							JavaScript. Hydrates the islands, keeps the signals, memos, effects and computations, and who
							owns what, runs a hole's Roc again when what it read changes, patches its node, runs effects once
							the page shows their markup, starts timers, sends requests and forms, and listens to streams.
						</p>
					</ArchBox>,
				]}
				wire="ids and strings"
				right={[
					<ArchBox kind="wasm" label="One wasm module">
						<ArchBox kind="zig" name={[<code>web/host/host.zig</code>]}>
							<p>Zig, inside the wasm: Roc's memory, and each of the platform's effects passed on to the runtime.</p>
						</ArchBox>
						<ArchBox kind="roc" name={[<>{"The app, on "}</>, <code>web/platform</code>]}>
							<p>
								Roc: the components, their markup with holes as closures, escaping and safe URLs, server
								functions as requests (<code>Html</code>, <code>Solid</code>, <code>Show</code>,
								<code>For</code>, <code>Loading</code>, <code>Errored</code>, <code>Context</code>,
								<code>Island</code>, <code>Server</code>, <code>Call</code>).
							</p>
						</ArchBox>
					</ArchBox>,
				]}
			/>
			<p>
				A component's markup reaches the runtime as JSON: elements, text, and the holes that read signals,
				each a Roc closure the runtime keeps as a boxed value by id. Running a hole is calling the app's
				<code>run</code> with that box; its answer is JSON again, a text, an attribute's value, or markup.
				The server platform renders the same JSON natively (<a href={"${Paths.platform_server}#islands"}>islands, rendered here</a>).
			</p>

			<Heading id="header">The header</Heading>
			<p>What an app gives the platform, and what the platform gives the host:</p>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_requires.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_requires.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				<code>main!</code> is the islands' root; <code>answer!</code> answers calls to server functions,
				for a runtime that renders on a server (Node, in the tests: the browser's copy answers none). There,
				a component's call is answered by it, in-process, as the server platform's routes answer it. The
				host provides three functions: mount the root, run a kept box, answer a call. The rest of the
				header is what Roc may ask of the runtime: keep a box, make, read or write a signal, make or read a
				memo, call a server function, fetch, listen to a stream, provide or find a context, where the page
				is (<code>Router.location!</code>), and the like. Each is a call into JavaScript.
			</p>
			<details>
				<summary>The whole header: <code>{Sources.platform_header.file}</code></summary>
				<Code title={Sources.platform_header.file} source={Sources.platform_header.rocx} />
			</details>

			<Heading id="click">One click, all the way</Heading>
			<p>You press <b>+</b> on the counter on the <a href={Paths.root}>front page</a>. Then:</p>
			<Trace>
				<li>
					<Who kind="js">JavaScript</Who> The button's listener, bound when the island hydrated,
					has the id of the handler's box. The runtime opens a turn and asks the host to run it.
				</li>
				<li>
					<Who kind="roc">Roc</Who> <code>{"|| set_count!(count!() + 1)"}</code> runs:
					<code>count!()</code> asks the runtime for the signal's value (3), and <code>set_count!</code>
					hands it the next (4), boxed.
				</li>
				<li>
					<Who kind="js">JavaScript</Who> The handler done, its writes apply: the signal holds 4,
					and the three computations that read it are due: the <code>{"<output>"}</code>'s text, the
					<code>negative</code> class, the Reset button's <code>disabled</code>.
				</li>
				<li>
					<Who kind="roc">Roc</Who> Each runs again: the text is <code>"4"</code>, the class still
					off, <code>disabled</code> still false.
				</li>
				<li>
					<Who kind="js">JavaScript</Who> Only the text changed, so only its node is set; a value a
					node already has isn't a patch. Nothing else ran. (Reset to 0 would patch the button too.)
				</li>
			</Trace>

			<Heading id="hydration">Hydration</Heading>
			<Trace>
				<li>
					<Who kind="js">JavaScript</Who> The site's script finds the page's
					<code>{"<rocx-island>"}</code> elements. A page with none stops here, and loads no wasm.
				</li>
				<li>
					<Who kind="js">JavaScript</Who> It fetches and compiles the wasm
					({Facts.fact!("client_brotli")} with brotli, for this site's islands), once for every island on the page,
					and reads the answers the server put at the end of it.
				</li>
				<li>
					<Who kind="roc">Roc</Who> The root runs; each <code>{"<Island name>"}</code> the page has
					a place for runs what is inside it, components and all.
				</li>
				<li>
					<Who kind="js">JavaScript</Who> The runtime walks the server's nodes beside the markup
					Roc made, and binds each computation to its own node: comments mark where each computed hole,
					row and <code>{"<Show>"}</code> is (<code>{"<!--x-->…<!--/x-->"}</code>). A server function asked for an
					answer the page has gets it from there, not over the network.
				</li>
				<li>
					<Who kind="js">JavaScript</Who> An island whose markup isn't what its components make
					(the HTML parser moved a <code>{"<div>"}</code> out of a <code>{"<p>"}</code>) is rendered again,
					alone, with a warning. The rest keep the server's nodes.
				</li>
			</Trace>

			<Heading id="waiting">Waiting, in wasm</Heading>
			<p>
				A memo waiting for the server is read as a plain value. Until it is in, the read throws, in
				JavaScript, through the Roc frames that asked: the runtime catches it where it ran the computation,
				puts Roc's stack pointer back where it was, and runs the computation again when the answer comes.
				No JSPI, no async in Roc. A <code>{"<Loading>"}</code> above shows its fallback meanwhile, the first
				time; after that, what was shown stays until the new value is in.
			</p>

			<Heading id="numbers">The numbers</Heading>
			<p>
				Measured by <code>site/build.mjs</code> in Node {Facts.fact!("node")}: this site's islands, built three
				ways (the other two on {Facts.fact!("compared")}, when <code>node site/build.mjs</code> last compared them).
				Rendering is the eight islands that need no server, every component and hole run once, as a server
				would, by the wasm, after warming up.
			</p>
			<TableWrap>
				<table class="compare">
					<thead><tr><th>Built with</th><th>Raw</th><th>gzip</th><th>brotli</th><th>Rendering</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td><code>--opt=size</code></td><td>{Facts.fact!("size_bytes")}</td><td>{Facts.fact!("size_gzip")}</td><td>{Facts.fact!("size_brotli")}</td><td>{Facts.fact!("size_render_us")} µs</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>--opt=speed</code></td><td>{Facts.fact!("speed_bytes")}</td><td>{Facts.fact!("speed_gzip")}</td><td>{Facts.fact!("speed_brotli")}</td><td>{Facts.fact!("speed_render_us")} µs</td></tr>
						<tr class="chosen"><td><code>--opt=size --specialize=yes</code> <span class="chosen-mark">this site</span></td><td>{Facts.fact!("client_bytes")}</td><td>{Facts.fact!("client_gzip")}</td><td>{Facts.fact!("client_brotli")}</td><td>{Facts.fact!("client_render_us")} µs</td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>
			<p>
				Specializing Roc's lambda sets makes this site's islands the smallest download of the three;
				<code>--opt=speed</code> renders a little faster, and is the biggest raw. Compiling this site's
				wasm took {Facts.fact!("client_compile_ms")} ms in Node. The runtime is
				{Facts.fact!("runtime_gzip")} of JavaScript, gzipped. The <a href={Paths.showcase}>showcase</a> measures
				itself as it runs: frames a second, and what each costs, Roc and the patches together.
			</p>
			<p>
				What weighs most is Roc's float code. Parsing a float from a string costs about 18 KB
				compressed, printing one about 12 KB, and sine and cosine about 5 KB, measured by building
				the islands with and without them. So the runtime sends frame times as whole microseconds,
				the flock prints its positions with whole numbers, and a bird's heading is a random vector
				scaled by a square root, which is a single wasm instruction.
			</p>
			<Callout kind="note" title="For scale">
				<p>A frame at 60 Hz is 16,667 µs.</p>
			</Callout>

			<Heading id="memory">Memory</Heading>
			<p>
				A page keeps its wasm instance, so memory has to come back. The host allocates through Roc's
				allocator and counts what is live. A box the runtime keeps (a hole's closure, a signal's value) is
				released when what made it goes: a row dropped, a <code>{"<Show>"}</code> switched, a value
				replaced. The tests click, type, add and drop rows, and render and hydrate islands again and again,
				and check that the count of live allocations doesn't move.
			</p>
		</NoHydration>
	</>
}

<style>
	/* The builds compared: numbers line up, and the site's is marked. */
	.compare td,
	.compare th {
		font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
		white-space: nowrap;
	}

	.compare td:first-child {
		white-space: normal;
	}

	.compare tr.chosen td {
		background: var(--accent-soft);
		font-weight: 600;
	}

	.chosen-mark {
		display: inline-block;
		padding: 0 var(--space-2);
		border-radius: var(--pill);
		background: var(--accent);
		box-shadow: var(--glow);
		color: var(--accent-text);
		font-size: var(--size-2xs);
		font-weight: 700;
	}
</style>
